hey everyone bandit here today i've got  something a little special for you it's  um  it's a picture of what i used to look  like when i was younger wasn't i so cute  what's that you don't think this was me  back in the day  oh is it because it looks completely  different and is an entirely different  species than what i am  if you didn't think this thing was me  because spoiler alert it wasn't then  chances are that you've probably  wondered at some point in your zelda  fandom about moblins for instance you  know those tall lanky long pig-nosed  looking enemies from breath of the wild  used to be tall buff stub-nosed enemies  from ocarina of time or even the  original legend of zelda  you see what i mean it doesn't make  sense and this applies to several other  enemies as well like the wiz robes or  the piccolons or the keys and while i'm  sure that in the real world these  differences are probably just due to  different designers over the years  designing different things or maybe even  different teams entirely but what  nintendo and co have designed outside in  the real world about the world of zelda  has become canon within the world of  zelda that's how canon works if it  wasn't then literally nothing would be  canon so this begs the question of  course canonically speaking lore wise  what gives why do enemies like the  moblins look so vastly different  depending on what point in the series  you're looking at them i'm so glad you  asked i made a video explaining why  you're watching it right now so be sure  to like this video if you like this  video don't like this video if you don't  and subscribe if you haven't already you  won't regret it anyway here we go so  let's take a closer look real quick at  some zelda enemies and how different  they've looked over the years like i  mentioned earlier one of the most  obviously changed enemies is the moblin  which is an enemy that's been a staple  in the zelda franchise all the way back  to the original legend of zelda in the  beginning the moblins were somewhat  bulldog-like in their faces like stubby  nose flappy jowls and floppy ears and  big teeth the works but later on in a  link to the past the moblin enemies were  redesigned to have pig-like faces  instead of dog-like faces and they only  appeared within the dark world which for  those of you who know could have  explained the appearance difference  since the dark world actually changes  the physical appearance of whoever  enters the dark world but thanks to a  later entry into the series we know that  this is not a possible explanation but  i'll get into that later on in the video  dog moblins or dog blinds as i like to  call them made a reappearance in the  next game link's awakening alongside the  newer pig-faced moblins called borblins  and that is their official name i didn't  make that up which was the pig version  from the previous game a link to the  past what's interesting is that both of  these enemies were present at the same  time in the same game but strangely  enough when the remake came out for the  nintendo switch there were no dog blinds  anymore all of them had been redesigned  to just fit the pig-like appearance of  the bourbons and i would try to explain  this discrepancy stuff but  it doesn't matter anyway because spoiler  alert it was all a dream so while their  appearance does matter it really doesn't  though moving on the moblins from  ocarina of time which did not take place  inside a dream revert back to their dog  blin original appearance but yet in wind  waker they're pig-like again in the four  swords they were pigs in twilight  princess they actually they didn't make  an appearance in twilight princess in  skyward sword they're neither i think  and finally currently in breath of the  wild they are back to their pig-like  appearances just with long anteater  noses for some reason now the maublin's  name in japanese is actually moriburin  which is a combination of two words mori  and gurberen  which literally translates to forest  goblin now normally in fantasy worlds  such as the legend of zelda goblins are  a species like you don't have multiple  kinds of goblins within the same  universe except for if you're talking  about middle earth because in the middle  earth franchise goblins are honestly  just humanoid monsters and their  specific features like facial structure  body size and structure is really  changeable honestly if you're watching  the lord of the rings or the hobbit and  you see an ugly monster chances are it's  a goblin or an orc which is the same  thing maybe depends on who you ask  anyway the point is normally a goblin is  just one specific kind of monster right  but since moblins and the legend of  zelda timeline go from being undefined  in skyward sword to pig like in four  swords to dog like an ocarina of time to  pig like and then to doglike and then to  pig like again with a weird long nose  this implies that the reasons for their  differences isn't just evolution because  they're flip-flopping back and forth and  we know that the pig-like moblins can't  be just dog blinds that went to the dark  world and came back because the first  time we see pig-like moblins as in four  swords before the dark world which  ganondorf created after the events of  ocarina of time after which he got the  full triforce of power  that was sucky let me do that again  because ganondorf created the dark world  after ocarina of time after he had  obtained the full triforce within the  sacred realm and we see the pig moblins  in four swords before ocarina of time  before the dark world and you could say  that both boar blinds and dog blinds are  the same species but just slight  variations within the same species  because they're both humanoid and walk  on two feet but in my opinion that's  like saying dogs and pigs are the same  species because they walk on four legs  in regards to their taxonomy dogs and  pigs share the same classes but after  that they're entirely different  so literally dogs are as similar to pigs  as humans are  so yeah that doesn't make sense to me  what i'm trying to say with all of this  is that doglands and bourbons are two  different species of monster that are  both referred to as mablin and moblins  aren't the only monsters in the zelda  franchise that this weird species  confusion has happened to for the sake  of time i won't go into the same amounts  of detail for the other species of  monsters but take wizrubs for example  originally they were just undead humans  all the way up until wind waker where  they randomly appeared like bird-like  humanoids which i guess could mean that  their undead rito huh that would be an  interesting theory bokoblins which were  introduced to the series in the wind  waker were initially pig-like with long  ears and pig snouts and a horn and they  showed signs of intelligence with their  clothing and weapons and then they  became i guess just classic goblin-like  in twilight princess with hair but then  they lost their horns and then in  skyward sword they got their horns back  kept their hair and in breath of the  wild currently they have no hair with a  single horn and they're apparently of  lower intelligence because they go  around with just crude weapons and very  little clothing which is clearly a sign  of low intelligence and keys have  clearly lost something over the years  now i could go on to other species of  enemies but i think i've made my case  quite clear the species of monsters in  the entire zelda franchise have been  shown to be completely different  depending on what point of the timeline  you're looking at them lore wise i think  this only has one possible explanation  that fits every piece i just talked  about see some people assume that ganon  was responsible for creating all the  monsters across the entire legend of  zelda franchise this notion does have  some merit to it because whenever you  kill a monster it just kind of explodes  normally into this purple mist of malice  but personally i think it has a  different much simpler explanation the  terms moblin or bacoblin or even wizrobe  could potentially refer not to that  monster's species but rather to the rank  that that monster holds within ganon's  army the monsters themselves are just  simply different species of beings  within the universe of the legend of  zelda i would call it hyrule by the way  but there are clearly lands outside  hyrule due to several sources from  in-game content anyway these different  monster species are recruited and then  given a rank and title name according to  their abilities the foot soldiers that  are good at swinging weapons but not  much else are called bokoblins the ones  that are a little bit bigger than the  bokoblins and can swing bigger weapons  are called moblins the variations that  are dead and brought back to life by  ganon's magic or wiz rogue magic are  called stall and then whatever enemy  they are magic users are called wiz  robes and so on and so forth now some  enemies like lionel's for instance have  always had the same appearance across  the franchise so yes their species could  be referred to as lionel there's no  evidence against that but for most of  the other enemies out there i think this  rank terminology appears to be the only  explanation now i could end the video  right there and ask you guys what you  think about it but i'd like to go a  little bit further into the realm of  just speculation and hopes of what we  might see in the future see if the enemy  names are really just referring to the  rank of that specific kind of monster  within ganon's army that must mean that  there are some of these species of  monsters roaming around out there that  are not a part of ganon's army right  meaning that there could be tribes of  bakoblins or whatever their real names  are just out there in the woods or maybe  in their own little villages as neutral  or even friendly parties that we have  yet to meet and what if one day be it in  breath of the wild 2 or the next zelda  game on down the line should it ever  arrive we eventually meet some of these  friendly monsters and befriend them that  would be pretty cool and i know that at  least kilton would be into the idea but  until then this has all been just my  theory what did you guys think are the  terms moblin and bacoblin just the ranks  of the monsters in ganon's army since  those monsters have been shown to be  entirely different species across the  franchise or does it literally not  matter because it was just different  designers having a different go at the  same kind of enemy let me know what you  think in the comments below also thank  you so much for watching huge thanks as  always to my bandit crew who make my day  every single day and please say hello to  the following new people welcome to  george m eric p alex s pineus and gamer  world who have all joined the ranks of  my elite if you watching are interested  in doing the same then that join button  is located right below any of my videos  or you can press that subscribe button  with the bells on that's just as good  but anyways that's all i've got for this  one so as always i hope you have a  wonderful rest of your day this is  bandit looking forward to seeing you in  the next one and signing out  [Music]  you

